css:
/* The second half of a panel broken over a column boundary.

   It keeps the panel's frame and colour — it is still the सूत्र panel,
   and stripping the frame made the rows look like loose formulas that
   had escaped their box. Only the HEADING goes: a reader does not need
   telling twice, and the open top edge plus the dashed rule say
   "continued" on their own. */
.fcard-cont { padding-top:9px; margin-top:0;
              border-top-left-radius:0; border-top-right-radius:0; }
.fcard-cont > .frow:first-child { border-top:1px dashed #cfc8b8;
              padding-top:8px; }

/* Room for a vector arrow inside a boxed formula.

   `.vec` draws its arrow with a pseudo-element .66em above the letter,
   and a boxed formula sits tight to its text — so the arrow over the
   first symbol was clipped by the box's own top edge. Adding padding
   alone was not enough: the arrow still reached past it, so it is
   tucked and shrunk here as well, the same way `.dm` does it.

   `overflow:visible` matters too — without it the box clips the
   pseudo-element regardless of the padding. */
.fx { padding-top:11px; overflow:visible; }
.fx .vec::after, .frow .vec::after { top:-0.5em; font-size:0.5em;
        overflow:visible; }
